WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Hey everyone, this is Kintsugi, built for the AI Revenue Recovery Track.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.800
Now before I show you guys what happens in this web simulation, let me just briefly explain

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.640
the problem at hand.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.120
In India, when people make online payments, a huge number of them fail.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.600
But most of the time, it's not due to the customer not having enough money.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.080
It's usually something like technical friction.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:33.960
For example, if HDFC servers get slow for around 5 minutes during lunch, or maybe it's somebody's

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.760
monthly subscription that tries to charge them on the 29th, but their salary doesn't

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.920
arrive until the 1st.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.000
Or maybe it's just a bank app timeout.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.160
Normally payment gateways just give up and show a red 'Payment Failed' error.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.160
The customer then leaves and the business loses around 20 to 30 percent of their sales.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.000
Kintsugi here solves this, and listens to payment errors.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.040
The second they happen, it figures out what exactly went wrong, and automatically recovers

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.040
the money.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.440
Okay, let's see it working live.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:22.040
Right now we have over ₹14 Lakhs at risk running this batch recovery.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:28.200
You can see how Kintsugi looks at each problem in real time and applies the best fix.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.560
Let's say the bank switch is slow.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.880
It switches it to a working route.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:40.320
If the auto-debit failed at the end of the month, it reschedules it for salary day.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.920
If somebody dropped out of checkout, it sends them a simple 1-tap WhatsApp link.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.640
You can see how this graph is climbing.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.640
The green curve is climbing as each payment is being recovered.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:02.680
You can see how it's reached up to ₹6 Lakhs in revenue recovered from ₹14 Lakhs at risk,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.200
close to 50 percent.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.480
This bar showcases where exactly the problems were and what fix we applied.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:17.480
Now let's understand what happens behind the scenes.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:23.880
I will take a look at this payment over here with an NPCI U30, which essentially means

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.720
that the payment didn't go through.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:33.760
Instead of asking the customer to try the payment all over again, Kintsugi automatically

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:40.320
reroutes it to a healthy bank switch and the money goes through.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.560
And let's look at this over here: for dropped payments, it instantly sends a 1-tap WhatsApp

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:55.160
link that brings them back to where they were and helps them complete checkout.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.920
Looking at any payment over here, you can notice how there is a 100 percent complete audit

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:07.920
log that shows what exactly happened, when it exactly happened, and what was implemented

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.080
in order to fix it—which is really convenient for anybody in the FinOps team to understand

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.680
what happened.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.160
Now safety is just as important as recovery.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.240
We can't have an automated system that consistently spams customers.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:29.920
Notice how this payment over here says HALTED (DND / POLICY).

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.240
This is because the customer has replied STOP to our messages.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:43.480
We've even set a hard rule that no more than 2 messages can be sent in 48 hours.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:49.360
Looking at this payment here for ₹1.5 Lakhs: for high-value payments like this, AI

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.800
doesn't touch the money directly or automatically.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.800
It prepares the settlement, but pauses at a FinOps approval gate so that a real

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.720
person in the team can then review it and sign off.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.520
This is the Guardrails tab.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:12.560
Over here we have the ability to set constraints however we want, which the system enforces

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:13.560
automatically.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.800
Now let's test an unexpected bank outage.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.200
I will click on this 'Simulate HDFC Spike'.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.040
We can see how the red graph has increased.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:33.280
Three new failures totaling around 70K rupees have been injected into the

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.280
queue.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:41.600
Kintsugi flags this immediately, isolates the degraded bank route, and starts protecting those transactions

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.600
from drops.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.360
As we can see here in real time, it has recovered essentially all the money that was almost

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.680
lost.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:57.640
To wrap up, Kintsugi takes dropped transactions back into real revenue.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:06.120
It gives merchants measurable money recovered, zero customer spam, and a complete audit trail.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:11.840
This is the architecture of the 4-stage pipeline that Kintsugi uses.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.760
And here is a comparison of Kintsugi against legacy payment recovery

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:18.760
systems.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:19.800
Thank you.
